DIV{HEADER1{accumulate,  -- apply a binary operator repeatedly}, UL{LI{DL{class => element, DT{Usage: }, DD{CODE{accumulate(f, x, L), class => language-macaulay2}}, DD{CODE{accumulate(f, L), class => language-macaulay2}}, DD{CODE{accumulate(L, x, f), class => language-macaulay2}}, DD{CODE{accumulate(L, f), class => language-macaulay2}}}}, LI{Inputs:, UL{LI{SPAN{TT{f}, , , SPAN{a , TO2{Macaulay2Doc :: Function, function}}, , }}, LI{SPAN{TT{x}, , , SPAN{a , TO2{Macaulay2Doc :: Thing, thing}}, , }}, LI{SPAN{TT{L}, , , SPAN{a , TO2{Macaulay2Doc :: VisibleList, visible list}}, , , TEX{or an instance of a class with the }, TO{Macaulay2Doc :: iterator}, TEX{ method installed}}}}}, LI{Outputs:, UL{LI{SPAN{TT{M}, , , SPAN{a , TO2{Macaulay2Doc :: List, list}}, , }}}}}, DIV{HEADER2{Description}, DIV{PARA{TEX{Suppose , TT{L=\{x0, x1, ..., xn\}}, . Then for any binary operator , TT{f}, , , TT{accumulate(f, L)},  returns the list , TT{\{f(x0, x1), f(f(x0, x1), x2), ...\} }, . In other words, the binary operator is applied to the first two elements of , TT{L}, , then to that result along with the next unused element of , TT{L}, , and so forth.}}}, TABLE{class => examples, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i1 : accumulate(plus, {0,1,2,3,4,5})

